<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1808–1871</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez was a French naval officer and colonial administrator who explored and charted the West African coast in the 1830s and 1840s. As commander on the coast and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, he negotiated treaties with rulers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-bassam">Grand-Bassam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assinie">Assinie</a> and in Gabon that laid the foundations of French colonies there, and he helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a> in 1849. He later rose to vice-admiral and held senior commands in the Crimean and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian Wars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-edouard-bouet-willaumez.jpg" alt="Louis Édouard Bouët-Willaumez" width="250" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Disdéri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disd%C3%A9ri_-_Louis_%C3%89douard_Bou%C3%ABt-Willaumez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
